    "title": "CSS colour picker",
    "subtitle": "One colour, four ways to write it in CSS",
    "answer": {
      "headline": "Pick a colour with the hue, saturation, lightness and alpha controls, then copy it as hex, rgb(), hsl() or a custom property.",
      "body": [
        "The sliders work in **HSL**: hue is the angle on the colour wheel (0° red, 120° green, 240° blue), saturation is how far the colour is from grey, and lightness runs from black through the pure hue to white. Typing a hex code moves the sliders to match.",
        "Every output describes the same colour. **Hex** packs the red, green and blue channels into two hexadecimal digits each, with an optional fourth pair for alpha; `rgb()` lists the same channels as numbers from 0 to 255; `hsl()` keeps the three values you set. Alpha below 100% switches the outputs to their transparent forms.",
        "Use a custom property such as `--color-picked` when a colour repeats across a stylesheet: define it once on `:root` and read it with `var()`, so changing the theme means changing one line."
      ],
      "caveats": [
        "Copy needs clipboard access; when the browser refuses it, the value is selected so Ctrl+C or Cmd+C copies it."
      ]
    },
